      "Ct Scan Of Chest": {
        "technique": "Axial scans of the chest were performed without IV contrast material according to the standard protocol.\n\nCOMPARISION: None\n\nMDCT was performed through the whole chest using tube-modulated retrospective ECG triggering. Imaging reconstruction was performed using workstation for MPR and quantitative analysis.",
        "findings": "** Limitation due to non-contrast study\n\n- No calcified plaque along anterior aspect of ascending aorta and posteromedial aspect of upper ascending aorta. No calcification along right lateral aspect of ascending aorta.\n\n- No gross calcification along upper thoracic course of internal mammary arteries (IMAs).\n\n- Mild calcification along aortic arch, descending thoracic aorta and visualized abdominal artery and its branches without aneurysmal dilatation.\n\n- Cardiomegaly. Mild coronary calcification along RCA, LM, and LCX. No calcification at LAD and PDA. Minimal pericardial effusion at posteroinferior aspect, measured up to 1 cm in thickness.\n\n- Severe calcified aortic valve.\n\n- Aortic diameter measurement as described\n\n# Ascending aorta at T7 level: 36.4x34.5 mm\n\n# Aortic arch: 29.6x28.6 mm\n\n# Descending aorta at T7 level: 22.2x22.0 mm\n\n# Celiac origin: 20.1x20.1 mm\n\n# SMA origin: 20.1x18.2 mm\n\nADDITIONAL FINDINGS: ** Limitation due to non-contrast study\n\n- Enlarge pulmonary trunk, diameter 3.9 cm\n\n- No enlargement of visualized thyroid gland.\n\n- Few small subpleural nodules at anterior and posterior basal segments of RLL, nonspecific.\n\n- Few sub-solid nodules at apicoposterior segment of LUL, size 0.4 cm (IM89, SE2) and posterior basal segment of LLL, size 0.4 cm (IM175, SE2), please follow up. A 2.3 cm bullae at apical segment of RUL (IM67, SE2).\n\n- No pleural effusion is noted.\n\n- Normal size and attenuation of liver parenchyma.\n\n- No distended gallbladder without calcified gallstones\n\n- No biliary tract dilatation.\n\n- Degenerative change of visualized spine without destructive bone lesion.\n\n1. Situs ambiguous with left isomerism, D-loop ventricle, situs solitis {A, D, S} are noted.\n\n2. Right SVC drains to RA, Left SVC drains to coronary sinus and then drains to RA, Interrupted IVC with dilated azygos vein continuation drains to RA. All pulmonary veins drain to new LA.\n\n3. S/P atrial septation with a thin septation separates old large RA into RA and new LA, which all PVs drain to new LA.\n\nDisproportion between PV ostium and ASD out flow tract is seen, suggestive pulmonary venous obstruction.\n\n AV concordance is noted.\n\n4. S/P VSD closure with small defect VSD below aortic valve is seen, about 4.1 mm.\n\n5. RV gives MPA and LV gives aorta (VA concordance). Aorta is located at right posterolateral to PA.\n\n6. Left dominance coronary artery. RCA and LMCA originate from left cusp. Separated acute marginal branch of RCA arises from right cusp and traversing along RV free wall.\n\n7. Right side aortic arch is noted.\n\n8. No PS is seen.\n\n9. No PDA is noted.\n\n10. Diffuse interlobular septal thickening with ground glass opacity prominent in BLL. Multifocal consolidation in both lungs, suggestive of infection or pulmonary edema.\n\n11. Enlarged MPA (MPA>Ao)\n\n11. Minimal pericardial effusion.\n\n12. NG tube cannot identify its tip. ETT with its tip 4.7 mm above carina.\n\n13 No hemivertebra or fused vertebra.\n\nMeasurements\n\n- Aorta: aortic annulus 12.6x8.7 mm, aortic sinus 15.9x15.5x15.4 mm, sinotubular junction 16.1x14.3 mm, ascending thoracic aorta 14.6x14.5 mm, aortic arch 9.6x8.7 mm, descending thoracic aorta at diaphragm 7.1x6.7 mm.\n\n- Pulmonary: pulmonary annulus 17.8x15.7 mm, MPA 26.6x23.8 mm, RPA 15.6x14.8 mm and LPA 18.1x17.9 mm.\n\n- Mcgoon ratio 4.75",
        "impression": "- Severe calcified aortic valve.\n\n- No calcification along ascending aorta.\n\n- Cardiomegaly. Mild coronary calcification along RCA, LM, and LCX. Minimal pericardial effusion at posteroinferior aspect, measured up to 1 cm in thickness.\n\n- Enlarge pulmonary trunk, diameter 3.9 cm\n\n- Few sub-solid nodules at apicoposterior segment of LUL, size 0.4 cm (IM89, SE2) and posterior basal segment of LLL, size 0.4 cm (IM175, SE2), please follow up.\n\n- A 2.3 cm bullae at apical segment of RUL (IM67, SE2).\n\nCT cardiac\n\nHN [redacted]\n\nCARDIAC CT ([date])\n\n1. Disproportion between PV ostium and ASD out flow tract is seen, suggestive pulmonary venous obstruction.\n\n2. Situs ambiguous with left isomerism, double SVC drain to RA. Interrupted IVC with dilated azygos vein continuation drains to RA.\n\n3. S/P atrial septation with a thin septation separates old large RA into RA and new LA, which all PVs drain to new LA. All pulmonary veins drain to new LA.\n\n4. S/P VSD closure with small defect VSD below aortic valve is seen, about 4.1 mm.\n\n5. Left dominance coronary artery. RCA and LMCA originate from left cusp. Separated acute marginal branch of RCA arises from right cusp and traversing along RV free wall.\n\n6. Pulmonary infection or pulmonary edema. Enlarged MPA (MPA>Ao).\n\n* Correlation with echocardiography will be helpful."
      }
